source: https://youtu.be/Td-sOB-cvgQ
yield: one rolled belly — Fallow's "level 2"
ingredients:
  - item: pork belly
    note: skin on, boneless, whole piece — weight not specified in the source; see notes on timing
components:
  - name: marinade
    ingredients:
      - item: fennel seeds
        note: toasted, then ground
      - item: rosemary
        note: picked and chopped
      - item: thyme
        note: picked and chopped
      - item: garlic
        note: finely grated
      - item: lemon zest
        note: grated, then chopped through the herbs to bind
      - item: salt
        note: generous — it seasons the whole way through and lightly cures the meat
      - item: black pepper
        note: go heavy
      - item: olive oil
        note: to bind to a paste
    steps:
      - toast the fennel seeds in a dry preheated pan until fragrant, then grind
      - chop the rosemary and thyme
      - grate the garlic and lemon zest, chop them through the herbs
      - fennel, herbs, garlic and zest together, then the salt and a heavy hit of pepper
      - bind with olive oil to a paste consistency
  - name: prep and roll
    ingredients:
      - item: fine salt
        note: for the skin, at the last moment
    steps:
      - score the skin first, before butterflying — much easier to do on a flat unrolled piece
      - butterfly the belly open to give the marinade more surface area to work on
      - spread the marinade over the meat side only, keeping the skin clean
      - roll up tight from one long edge
      - tie with butcher's twine along the length; it comes off after cooking
      - fine salt all over the skin so it has maximum opportunity to crisp
      - cook straight away — no resting or marinating time in this version
  - name: cook
    steps:
      - oven at 160°C, 1.5 hrs (set a timer for an hour and check)
      - up to 220°C for a final 20 mins to blister the skin
      - rest before slicing, then cut the twine off
  - name: serving
    steps:
      - warm potato salad — potatoes dressed with olive oil, salt and pepper
      - apple sauce
      - a little pork demiglace over
      - olive oil to finish for shine
notes: |
  Fallow's level 2, from the "pork belly in 3 levels" video. The whole thing is one session —
  no brine, no overnight anything, which is what separates it from most porchetta recipes.

  Fennel, rosemary and garlic are the constant. Everything else in a porchetta mix moves
  around region to region, but those three are always there — start from them and improvise
  the rest.

  Score the skin before you butterfly. In the video Jack forgets and has to score the rolled
  joint, which works but is fiddly and easy to cut unevenly. Scoring opens paths for heat,
  salt and oil to get into the skin and puff it.

  Skin salt goes on last and dry. Keep the marinade off the skin entirely — it's wet, oily
  and full of chopped herbs, all of which stop the skin drying out and crisping, and the
  herbs will scorch at 220°C.

  The salt in the marinade is doing double duty. It seasons the interior of a thick rolled
  joint, which nothing applied to the outside can reach, and it firms the surface of the meat
  as it sits — a very light cure that helps the roll hold its shape.

  Timing is thickness-driven, not weight-driven. The source gives 1.5 hrs at 160°C plus 20
  mins at 220°C without stating a belly weight, so treat those as the numbers for a
  standard-thickness rolled belly and go by feel if yours is fatter or thinner. Rolling is
  what makes it slow — you've turned a 4cm slab into a 10cm cylinder and the heat has to get
  to the middle.

  The 220°C blast is the whole game and it's also where it goes wrong. Watch it, don't trust
  the 20 mins — the twine, the exposed herb paste at the ends and the rendered fat in the tray
  will all catch before the skin does if the oven runs hot.

  Apple sauce is not optional garnish. It's a very rich, very fatty joint and the acid is what
  makes a second slice possible — same logic as the vinegar sauce on pulled-pork.
